  "hints": [
    "The rank of `x` is the number of distinct values smaller than `x`. How do you list the distinct values in order?",
    "Sort the array, remove duplicates, and the rank of `x` is its index in that list.",
    "Find the index with binary search (or a hash map built from the unique list)."
  ],
  "editorial": "Sort a copy of the array and drop duplicates to get `u`. The rank of `x` is `lower_bound(u, x)`, its position in `u`; a hash map `value -> index` gives the same in `O(1)` expected. Sorting costs `O(n log n)` and each lookup `O(log n)`, total `O(n log n)`.\n\nThe common bug is forgetting to deduplicate (ranks then skip numbers) or using the position in the sorted array without removing equal values.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
